  "reason": "The records collectively make the later study plausibly reconstructible, although no single record discloses the complete coating–substrate nanoindentation/friction simulation. R1 and especially R13 provide molecular-dynamics evidence in a closely related FCC CoNiCrFeMn HEA that increasing temperature lowers dislocation density, promotes dislocation annihilation or unlocking, increases the HCP fraction, and changes stacking-fault-mediated deformation. R10 independently links HCP formation in the same alloy family to stacking faults. R14 and R15 support elevated-temperature stiffness or strength loss, while R12 supplies the HEA-film tribology context and R18 supplies nanoindentation-based hardness/modulus measurement. An expert could therefore combine temperature-controlled deformation simulation, nanoindentation/tribology of an HEA coating, and tracking of FCC/HCP order and dislocations to propose the later intervention and mechanism. The precise FeNiCrCoCu-on-Cu geometry, 300–1200 K schedule, virtual-indenter parameters, and sharp transition above about 900 K are not directly disclosed."
